EDGECLAW(1)                General Commands Manual                EDGECLAW(1)

NAME
     edgeclaw — AI-powered personal assistant agent with shell-like syntax

SYNOPSIS
     edgeclaw

DESCRIPTION
     EdgeClaw is an interactive terminal AI assistant offering bash-inspired
     task management.  It supports multi-turn conversations, tool calling,
     pipeline chaining, I/O redirection, variable expansion, and Unix-style
     job control for orchestrating multiple parallel agent tasks.

OPTIONS
     The interactive TUI takes no command-line arguments.  On first run,
     when config.json does not exist, it is created with defaults.

GETTING STARTED
           $ edgeclaw
           EdgeClaw TUI ready.

     On first run config.json is created with defaults and the guided setup
     wizard opens once (provider, API key, model); reopen it any time with
     /connect.  Type a message in the input box and press Enter: non-/ input
     goes through the shell parser (see SHELL SYNTAX) and becomes a task in
     the current foreground group.  Conversations are saved per session under
     workspace/<session_id>/.

     Type /help for the command list.

     While the input starts with /, a completion list hangs just below it:
     Up/Down move the selection, Tab completes it, Esc dismisses it.  Enter
     is untouched -- it still sends what you typed.

     Two keys interrupt things.  Esc stops the foreground group's running
     task (what /task stop does); while the completion list is open, Esc
     dismisses the list instead.  Ctrl+C clears the input box and shows
     "Ctrl+C again to quit" in the footer, in place of the working
     directory -- press it a second time (within ~1.5s) to quit EdgeClaw.

     The footer's right end reads "model · ctx used/window · sandbox:on|off
     · /help" (the context gauge is the foreground group's current context;
     a ~ marks an estimate), and while a task runs the bottom row becomes a
     progress bar carrying its elapsed time and an "Esc to stop" hint.

SHELL SYNTAX
     All non-/ input is processed through the edgeclaw shell parser:

     Implicit task creation
           > analyze src/ for bugs
       Creates a task in the current foreground group with the text as input.

     @ syntax (new group)
           > @review the latest commits
       Same as above but creates a new group for the task.

     Pipe (|)
           > scan for issues | fix them | write a report
       Each segment is a separate task, labeled [pipe].  Each task depends
       on the previous one.  When a task completes, its output is injected
       into the next task's LLM context.

     Sequential (;)
           > review src/ ; run tests ; summarize > report.md
       Splits on ; (outside quotes) and executes each sub-command in order.
       Each segment is labeled [seq].  Tasks wait for the previous one to
       complete, but output is NOT injected between segments (unlike |).

     Background (&)
           > run the full test suite &
       Runs the task in the background.  The prompt returns immediately.

     Output redirect (>  >>  2>)
           > analyze code > report.md
           > append log >> all.log
           > build 2> errors.log
       After the task completes, its output is written to the specified file.
       >> appends.  2> writes stderr.  Quoted > is not interpreted.

     Input redirect (<)
           > fix bugs < context.txt
       Reads context.txt and appends its content to the task's user input
       (if there is preceding text, it is joined with a newline separator).
       If the file does not exist or is a directory, the task is NOT created
       and an error message is shown instead.

     Task references ({{...}})
           > {{LAST}}
           > {{1}}
           > {{SELF}}
       {{LAST}}  — output of the most recently completed task (by
                   completion order, not creation order).
       {{N}}     — output of task with id N (e.g. {{1}}, {{2}}).
       {{SELF}}  — task id of the current foreground active task.
       The sigil is {{...}} and NOT $: $ belongs to the prompt template
       layer ($1, $@, ${1:-x} -- the pi / Claude Code / opencode spelling).
       Only those three forms count; any other {{...}} (a pasted
       {{ var }}, a stray brace) is copied verbatim, and so is every $.
       Expansion happens after pipe splitting and redirect stripping, so
       any | or > in the expanded output is treated as literal text.

     Local shell (!)
           > !git status
       A line starting with ! runs a local shell command on THIS machine. The command
       becomes a task of its own -- its own line in the sidebar, its own entry in the
       group's history -- whose only job is to issue one exec_shell call. No model is
       called, and it costs no tokens. Its output is that task's result and is stored
       in the group's history, so the next prompt sees the command and its output.
       The command runs through sh -c in the session's working directory, subject to
       the sandbox setting and the same output cap as the exec_shell tool, but with NO
       timeout: the command runs until it exits. Bound it yourself when you want to --
       !timeout 60 make -j8. Stop it with /task stop <id> (or /session stop), which kills
       the command's whole process group.
       !!  — run this session's previous ! command again.
       \!  — escape: send !cmd as ordinary input (a task description) instead.
       !   alone — usage.
       A command that needs a terminal (sudo, vim, ssh, an editor) fails immediately:
       the child gets no tty. A non-zero exit is NOT a task failure -- the output
       (including stderr) is still the result.
     History expansion
       There is none: ! is only special at the start of a line (the local
       shell above); a !n or !! inside a task description is literal text.

     Options on @ and /task new
           > @"input text" --bg -d task1 --group dev
       Options are recognised only after a quoted description (or when the
       description is the only content); otherwise a token starting with "-"
       is a syntax error, not a silently-ignored flag.
       --bg              Run in background.
       --new-group       Open a group of its own (a fresh conversation).
       -d <id>           Wait for this task before executing (repeatable).
       --group <id>      Create in this group (-g is a shortcut).

SHELL ARCHITECTURE
     Input processing follows a Unix-style pipeline:

       User Input → History Expansion → Tokenizer → Parser → AST → Executor

     Tokenizer
       A quote-aware tokenizer respecting " and ' quoting.  Token types:
       TOK_TEXT (regular text), TOK_PIPE (|), TOK_SEQ (;), TOK_BG (&),
       TOK_REDIR_OUT (>), TOK_REDIR_APPEND (>>), TOK_REDIR_ERR (2>),
       TOK_REDIR_IN (<).

     Parser & AST
       Recursive-descent parser building an AST (Abstract Syntax Tree):

         AST_ROOT   — top-level wrapper
           AST_SEQ  — ; chain (sequential dependency)
             AST_PIPE — | chain (pipe dependency with injection)
               AST_BG     — & background modifier
               AST_REDIR_* — > / >> / 2> / < redirection
               AST_CMD   — leaf node: text + options

       The executor walks the tree recursively via exec_ctx_t, carrying
       target_group (preserved across pipe segments), bg flag, and dep_type.

     Variable expansion (two-phase)
       Phase 1 (collect) — at task creation: validates {{...}} references,
       converts them to implicit dependencies, preserves the text as-is.
       Phase 2 (expand)  — when dependencies resolve: replaces the
       reference with the referenced task's actual output text, then
       sends to the LLM.  The sigil is {{...}}, not `$` (which belongs
       to prompt templates).
       Expansion happens after pipe splitting and redirect stripping,
       so any | or > in expanded output is treated as literal text.

     Dependency system
       Tasks form a DAG via dependency edges.  Each task maintains two
       linked lists: deps (waits for) and dependents (waited by).  DFS
       cycle detection runs on every new dependency to prevent deadlocks.

       Dependency types:
         JOIN      (|)       Source output injected into destination's LLM
                             context.  A source that fails / is cancelled /
                             interrupted cascades: the destination is
                             cancelled too, and the edge stays.
         SEQUENCE  (;)       Same waiting and cascade, but NO output
                             injection.

TASK MODEL
     Three-layer Unix-like process model:

     Task     — A conversation sharing history with sibling tasks.
                Like a Unix thread (shared address space).  Each task
                has a unique task_id (t1, t2, ...).

     Group    — An isolated container.  Only the active task outputs.
                Cross-group communication uses | pipe.
                Like a Unix process.  Named by leader task's id.

     Session  — Per-session history storage.  Like a Unix login session.
                Contains multiple groups in a linked list.

     Fork     — /task fork <parent> <desc> creates a child that leads a
                group of its own (taking the foreground) and inherits the
                parent group's memory plus a snapshot of its conversation.

TASK STATES
       IDLE → WAITING_DEPS → LLM_PROCESSING ⇄ TOOL_CALLING
                                            → COMPLETED / CANCELLED / ERROR
       INTERRUPTED (the loop stopped mid-flight)
       (WAITING_INPUT is retired: IDLE is the ready state, and there is no
       separate waiting-for-input phase.)

     LLM_PROCESSING and TOOL_CALLING alternate as tools are invoked.
     Tasks reach COMPLETED on final LLM response; CANCELLED when stopped;
     ERROR on failure.

     Restart behavior: on exit every unfinished task is rewritten as
     INTERRUPTED (queued ones included), and the interrupt propagates to
     dependents like a failure.  /task resume [tid] re-derives the state to
     return to:
       - unsatisfied deps            -> WAITING_DEPS
       - unfinished tool call        -> TOOL_CALLING (re-run not-yet-started
                                        tools; a mid-flight tool gets a
                                        synthesized "interrupted" result)
       - pending turn (no LLM reply) -> LLM_PROCESSING
       - otherwise                   -> IDLE

     Failed tasks: a task that failed mid-run is ERROR but keeps its
     half-finished turn (and any interrupted tool ledger).  /task resume
     [tid] continues that turn instead of starting over.

     Cancelled tasks: a task stopped with /task stop is CANCELLED but not
     destroyed -- its turn and messages are kept; /task resume revives it.

     - COMPLETED tasks are terminal; read output with /task output <id>.
     - WAITING_DEPS tasks accept input and queue it until deps resolve.
     - INTERRUPTED tasks are revived by /task resume.
     - SUSPENDED tasks are parked; /task resume revives.  Nothing produces
       this state today.
     - ERROR tasks failed mid-run; /task resume continues their unfinished turn.
     - CANCELLED tasks were stopped; /task resume continues them too.

SHELL-LIKE JOB CONTROL
     Shell            EdgeClaw
     ─────────────────────────────────────────
     cmd &            task "input" &  or  task "input" --bg
     cmd | cmd        a | b | c  (pipe chain, output injected)
     cmd ; cmd        a ; b      (sequential, no injection)
     cmd > file       task > out.md  (redirect output)
     cmd >> file      task >> log    (append output)
     cmd 2> file      task 2> err    (stderr redirect)
     cmd < file       task < in.txt  (input from file)
     jobs             /task list
     fg %n            /task fg <id>
     bg %n            /task bg [id]
     kill %n          /task stop <id>
     !cmd             !git status  (local shell: a task of its own)
     {{LAST}} {{1}} {{SELF}}   task references

COMMAND REFERENCE
     General
     /help                         Show the command list.
     /whoami                       Show the current session, model,
                                   foreground group, and active task.
     /model                        List the current provider's chat models
                                   (the current one marked).
     /model <id>                   Switch model (persisted).
     /model search <q>             Search chat models across all providers.
     /prompt                       List the templates in workspace/prompts/.
     /prompt <name> [args...]      Run a template: substitute its $1 / $@ /
                                   ${...} placeholders ({{...}} works too) and
                                   run the result as input.  The last
                                   placeholder takes the remaining args; a body
                                   with no placeholder gets the args appended.
     /skill                        List the skills (ours, plus any found in a
                                   .claude / .agents / .pi / .opencode
                                   skills directory).
     /skill <name> [args...]       Run a skill directly: its SKILL.md body goes
                                   into this task wrapped in a <skill name=...
                                   location=...> block (pi's shape, so the model
                                   knows where the skill's relative paths hang
                                   off); the args are substituted, or appended
                                   after the closing tag when the body has
                                   none.
     /reload                       Re-read the resource files and rebuild the
                                   cached system prompt (a skill or AGENTS.md
                                   edited on disk takes effect; config.json
                                   still needs a restart).
     /connect                      Guided setup (provider, API key, model).
     /sandbox [on|off]             Enable/disable the filesystem sandbox.
     /sandbox list                 Show the allow/deny path lists.
     /sandbox add-ro|rm-ro <dir>   Add/remove a read-only path.
     /sandbox add-rw|rm-rw <dir>   Add/remove a read-write path.
     /sandbox add-deny|rm-deny <dir>
                                   Add/remove a masked dir (wins over ro/rw).

     Session
     /session new [name] [dir]     Start a new session (optionally named,
                                   optionally with a working directory).
     /session switch <name_or_id>  Switch to another session.
     /session list                 List all sessions.
     /session info                 Show current session details.
     /session status [id]          Show a session's state (default: active).
     /session save                 Persist the current session's history
                                   and state.
     /session stop                 Cancel all active tasks in the current
                                   session.
     /session remove <name_or_id>  Remove a session (the last one cannot
                                   be removed).
     /session rename <old> <new>   Rename a session.
     /session memo [show [id]]     Show the session memory (all entries, or
                                   one entry's detail).
     /session memo search <q>      Search the session memory.
     /session memo curate [focus]  Curate durable entries out of the group
                                   memories now.
     /session memo compact         Compact the session memory now.

     Task creation
     /task new <desc>                  Create a task in the group in front.
     /task new <desc> --bg             Same group, background (no foreground).
     /task new <desc> --new-group      Open a group of its own (foreground).
     /task new <desc> -g <gid>         Create in existing group.
     /task new <desc> -d <tid>         Create + wait for dependency,
                                       injecting its output (--with is the
                                       explicit form).
     /task fork <pid> <desc>           Fork: child leads its own group and
                                       inherits the parent's context.

     Task inspection
     /task list               List all groups and tasks. Active task
                               marked with * (e.g. [L*] or [M*]).
     /task status [tid]       Show task details (state, output, deps).
     /task output <tid>       Show only the task's output text.
     /task history <tid>      Show the task's messages (--json = raw payload).

     Foreground / active
     /task fg <tid>           Foreground + set as active task.
     /task bg [tid]           Move the foreground group (or that task) to
                               the background.

     Task control
     /task stop [tid]         Cancel a task (SIGTERM — notifies deps).
                              Without an id, stops the active task of the
                              foreground group; errors if the id is not
                              found.
     /task resume [tid]       Resume an INTERRUPTED, SUSPENDED, ERROR or
                              CANCELLED task: re-derives state (deps / tool
                              chain / turn) and drives it; an ERROR or
                              CANCELLED task continues its unfinished turn.
                              Without an id, resumes the active task of the
                              foreground group.
     /task resume --all       Re-decide every stopped task of the session
                              (after a restart).

     Group
     /group list              List all process groups.
     /group info [id]         A group's context / fold numbers (default:
                              the foreground group).
     /group history <id>      The whole group's history, one block per member.
     /group fold [id] [focus] Fold the group now, ignoring the trigger
                              (default: the foreground group).

OUTPUT
     Only the session's active task outputs to the terminal.  Other tasks
     complete silently; use /task output <id> to read output.  /task fg
     changes the active task.

CONTEXT SHARING
     Within a group: all tasks share the full conversation history.

     Cross-task (a -d dependency, or a | pipe): source task's user
     inputs and final output are injected into the destination's LLM context
     as a Q&A-style system message:

         [子任务 analyze (id=1)]:
         Q: help me analyze src/ for bugs
         A: Found 3 performance issues ...

WORKFLOW EXAMPLES
     Simple conversation
           $ edgeclaw
           > help me understand this code
           Welcome! How can I help?

     @ syntax (new group + task)
           > @code "write a Python HTTP server"

     Pipe chain (output injected at each step)
           > scan for vulns | create fix plan | apply patches

     Sequential (no output injection)
           > review src/ ; run tests ; commit changes

     Sequential with redirect
           > review src/ ; run tests ; summarize findings > report.md

     Combined pipes + redirects
           > analyze codebase | find critical bugs | generate report > bugs.md
           > build project 2> build-errors.log
           > fix all issues < context.txt

     Background + pipeline
           > analyze the error logs &
           > audit API endpoints &
           > /task list
             - [fg] 1 (2)
                 - [L]* 1   LLM_PROCESSING  analyze the error logs
                 - [M]  2   LLM_PROCESSING  audit API endpoints
           > merge both results -d 1 -d 2 > merged.md

     Variable expansion (two-phase)
           > find performance bottlenecks in src/
           ... task 1 completes ...
           > based on {{LAST}}, create an optimization plan
           ... task 2 waits for task 1, then sees its output ...

     Fork (child leads its own group)
           > /task fork task-0 "audit the database schema"

FILES
     ~/.edgeclaw/                        EdgeClaw home directory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/config.json             Configuration (API key, model).
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/              Workspace root.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/<sid>/        Per-session directory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/<sid>/history.jsonl  Session history.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/skills/       Custom skill SKILL.md files.
     Prompts and skills are also read from the directories the other tools
     already use, the first hit winning: the project's (walking up from the
     session's directory -- .edgeclaw .claude .agents .opencode .pi), then
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace (ours, the only writable one), then the user's
     (~/.claude, ~/.agents, ~/.config/opencode, ~/.pi/agent).  A name found in
     two roots is REPORTED -- a log warning, and a "_(shadowed: ...)_" line in
     /prompt and /skill -- rather than dropped silently.

